#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "896ffdd3-8589-5928-a8bf-d20ba46df469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352766} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf46d83-25b4-5937-9aae-c7c27751b925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352788} {:answer/id #uuid "c001bb20-055e-50b5-be29-744ebf6c4a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353217} {:answer/id #uuid "c803bd64-f6a2-5364-b05c-5a37ac1de747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353306} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4d0092-8f74-5633-8113-3ce745643fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c001bb20-055e-50b5-be29-744ebf6c4a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353217} :answer-field/id #uuid "8adabeca-b826-598f-899a-01480745fc79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cefee34-6c96-52a2-810c-1fc692ea436d" :question/math-academy-id "q-86170" :question/problem "![](images/3e/3ec66e7d27a46ad991e1aa10281a9850c500ba649b9699834fd867247b665376.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =ab \\\\  & =5(1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-5.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,5].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)(4) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-5+10 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136111b7-0834-547c-80d0-1d009758dc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351734} {:answer/id #uuid "2a15147b-b82d-5d08-b257-0ff20f5f4993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351954} {:answer/id #uuid "6d99cb59-adab-577b-b0f7-e416a14ae926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352531} {:answer/id #uuid "cde67277-a7d8-51e5-baab-0c3c2114f90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353355} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7329cd-d17e-5495-bc47-87ef9fdd13e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d99cb59-adab-577b-b0f7-e416a14ae926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352531} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c4b64d-ca76-5776-939f-630ddca986ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eba3d729-fa27-507f-86e4-8cdb46533946" :question/math-academy-id "q-86174" :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a right triange, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+(4-\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2574980-7b3e-56b9-9bb5-816d7922784b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352957} {:answer/id #uuid "c4cf3c4c-27ba-5ebd-92c3-a4887a144a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353271} {:answer/id #uuid "e45864b2-aba3-5647-b30b-b33f87e22338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353559} {:answer/id #uuid "ea063350-e7f6-5fd1-bd2f-d066a7fbdb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353604} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9cef2c-3376-5342-98e4-920a5e325266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4cf3c4c-27ba-5ebd-92c3-a4887a144a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353271} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c54c8a-827e-5695-9838-346de9675d3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "805b2992-f685-5d7b-bccd-5900affa2a81" :question/math-academy-id "q-86178" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f9b82ba9e1ed640aedd6f67118beef2d88c419bbd046171cca5b527c2b407a5.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2]$, the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-2,2),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2).$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =4(3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =12-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6+12-2\\pi +3 \\\\  & =21-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c0277c-fb4b-5026-9fd8-6c7810e057b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351756} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6dd320-c836-51a4-ad23-8614799db41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351838} {:answer/id #uuid "5f17f867-8ede-528f-b531-5e234c5f7333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352410} {:answer/id #uuid "67375961-39e8-54d5-9054-515f282d6534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352482} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a357ac-56ed-5863-b3dc-01fa6b42eb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3a357ac-56ed-5863-b3dc-01fa6b42eb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353259} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d77c46d-1aa3-501f-aae2-671960790f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "682dd404-2e33-5194-9b75-83eececc2df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86350" :question/problem "The following table shows the velocity of a plane during a $20$-minute trip.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{kilometers/minute})$ | $17$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance traveled during this period has a value of  $450\\,\\text{km}$, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{v(5)+v(10)+v(15)+v(20)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+27+k+18)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5(k+70) \\\\  & =5k+350\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled during the $20$ minute period. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $450\\,\\text{km},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+350 & =450 \\\\ 5k & =450-350 \\\\ k & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0065af71-29a6-5ebd-9bb2-e8f6fd53309c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351566} {:answer/id #uuid "0e38c9a8-52aa-57b5-8cd2-d15086786ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351684} {:answer/id #uuid "20bda32c-1ae4-5791-b78f-9ded45871b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351857} {:answer/id #uuid "43179f12-d2ec-5fea-8ae8-1b1e8c829d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352184} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a0b89-b520-516a-984b-548f08bea2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 19 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e38c9a8-52aa-57b5-8cd2-d15086786ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351684} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ec5791-f08c-5efc-bb1c-b825849222ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa2ab21a-e8b5-56c0-b8a9-838a56ba099f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86676" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce48fd3-6d90-5867-bc8c-48f781a9a134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351673} {:answer/id #uuid "6dea34ce-51cc-54ce-bd9e-2e2c5ec68b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352535} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac80d5-5537-5ad5-953b-dc791c6b3dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352561} {:answer/id #uuid "7239e333-3699-5e0e-a679-3638ec2f4a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 21 & -32\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b23a21b-03eb-5d9a-8c5d-1e2dbf0ce327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -19 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce48fd3-6d90-5867-bc8c-48f781a9a134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351673} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ed20fe-9723-5a36-b174-23a11d61985b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1fcc8b2-84cd-574b-a993-68fbd5d3c967" :question/math-academy-id "q-86680" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0838f7e1-1e2a-5de4-899d-0534f10f1b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351641} {:answer/id #uuid "4bdae80f-03b9-55c3-aadd-11684fd45d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352262} {:answer/id #uuid "836126d0-86c5-54f1-8f69-77f8c1c3afc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352716} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a16fcf-857f-5b60-adf6-cb3cf7586045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353550} {:answer/id #uuid "f648b42e-29eb-56ba-be95-7cf583a892a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0838f7e1-1e2a-5de4-899d-0534f10f1b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351641} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f200b4-abd8-556b-96a6-d61eebe5f500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18c9e3a0-322a-5b85-8d72-26e41db87e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-86682" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{71},$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27359bfd-be3c-5cf1-bd01-de39941cd5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351924} {:answer/id #uuid "69b41b65-8c9f-57bf-a764-c4be901cae38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-60 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352504} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca878c6-3ac9-5a69-85fc-d735d7d1e6a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -28 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352524} {:answer/id #uuid "c65ad6da-b0e6-5d01-9b41-a20ca0d5bf88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 60 \\\\ -18 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353289} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c25710-3be0-58ab-8eca-84880e70270f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 6 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27359bfd-be3c-5cf1-bd01-de39941cd5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351924} :answer-field/id #uuid "11865484-c0f9-5b42-84f8-6f6395f0b142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c81d246-a69b-5179-85f7-88df79048f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86689" :question/problem "Find ${M}^{24},$ where\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{24} & =P{D}^{24}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{24}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[12]{2})^{24} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[8]{2})^{24}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 40 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b940dc-40c4-5587-99cb-a46b5669f00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351581} {:answer/id #uuid "36451ec8-9550-599f-9524-6efb083c9e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352073} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0011e3-3c09-55fb-a899-6fc1c02d871e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352377} {:answer/id #uuid "6678d030-b08a-54a4-8dbd-e35a3db5a4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352470} {:answer/id #uuid "92887453-53d2-5699-9d42-9b75a6b33c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92887453-53d2-5699-9d42-9b75a6b33c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352835} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e3561b1-f239-5584-a442-e885bcbde6bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d81f51f-fe75-524a-aa0f-42e04cbcf9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-86691" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}E=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE=\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121c30d3-381d-5eec-812d-224e51ec2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351718} {:answer/id #uuid "28b0ed5c-9277-577c-b0f4-46d45ce08e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351944} {:answer/id #uuid "82f7010b-3ded-54da-922f-5d2a3139d0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352711} {:answer/id #uuid "b584defc-90ec-51d9-af5c-7473d8c554a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353119} {:answer/id #uuid "c68fd66d-ff23-5aca-a7e5-22e416ac2158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121c30d3-381d-5eec-812d-224e51ec2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351718} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d0c1c4-db7a-5402-823c-10bcfe18c23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0ebddfd-78f1-5dda-8990-10394fcd7459" :question/math-academy-id "q-86693" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB=\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123147cc-29a9-5a9b-8197-5141287824db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351722} {:answer/id #uuid "13590b66-311d-5d93-824a-b8885d95a619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351733} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce46df1-f81c-53bf-9562-397b90e58c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352387} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e2a4f2-1115-53ef-a5a2-b66e4951afd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353452} {:answer/id #uuid "e2dc21a8-005f-55af-bc53-974ee66b05b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8e2a4f2-1115-53ef-a5a2-b66e4951afd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353452} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece55d7d-c7d4-5233-ba8f-7f279a6be42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5dc95dbe-f9a5-583f-bd3e-71e914c88035" :question/math-academy-id "q-86694" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}T=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT=\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45" :knowledge-point/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Perpendicular Bisector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0244a00d-4710-50e9-8997-212737b64b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351584} {:answer/id #uuid "27c69d55-1729-5939-a1f1-8d6f552d0aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351931} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebb7ae7-468c-57ec-9b4d-324812e14481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352406} {:answer/id #uuid "d99c9ed5-0a76-5cae-b60c-722ece8b8e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353457} {:answer/id #uuid "dae524f4-19b6-5fa4-8c85-43cf6c01b47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ebb7ae7-468c-57ec-9b4d-324812e14481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352406} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab93659-2867-5b81-aaea-c8668289e6de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c3f9e3b-ce11-530a-9baa-05d3b323a845" :question/math-academy-id "q-87502" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(1,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n \n\n![](images/05/052b405c104f0d6bc353782c6e07bfb1e0245726d86346a545ea38aa31941711.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(1,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x-(-2))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x+4 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-6y+9 \\\\ 6y & =-6x+10-4 \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(-x+1)^{2}-2x-4(-x+1)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1-2x+4x-4+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(0)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=-0+1=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45" :knowledge-point/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Perpendicular Bisector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b12f7b4-7350-5a21-b008-1a533954dcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352109} {:answer/id #uuid "abba52c0-814c-5b2f-9e2d-4874044e701a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353023} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c9b68d-8b43-58fe-895a-665afc0d4def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353300} {:answer/id #uuid "f5161e75-5690-5b58-8b4a-c973a4e037ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353707} {:answer/id #uuid "fe92d2b8-ef0e-5966-99eb-7bda1593f02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5161e75-5690-5b58-8b4a-c973a4e037ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353707} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ed4368-1a6a-5ad3-8782-0a36a45be756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b28c6e16-047f-5299-b8dc-57e7d48491a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-87515" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(2,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/8b/8be39e6dde084154ecfd43718cb76352c69d26bd76d520177a6c41ee69a7f18e.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(2,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-4y+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-8x+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4y+20 \\\\ 8y & =8x-16 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(x-2)^{2}-4x-4\\cdot (x-2)+8 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+4-4x-4x+8+8 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-12x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(3)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(3,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} {:answer/id #uuid "555a8298-192e-50be-8d44-14723e16f11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352323} {:answer/id #uuid "58619be6-c805-5344-9021-173a95976dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352344} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9db913-3c22-5c37-be9e-ca617e5d0dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353213} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c2b5b7-b8a6-51db-98b0-9f4ba4bb301f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd53aab-78d5-531e-a248-9e6ce0d0938b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31688145-09ed-5774-a522-d76e627c7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-88296" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =\\underbrace{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{4}+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a4042a-f605-574f-a228-ff4ae6d308e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352358} {:answer/id #uuid "73d447eb-1e81-51dc-b291-5725cee52d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352581} {:answer/id #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} {:answer/id #uuid "b27eb1ec-b060-5c68-b2a6-225457f0de0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353093} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c62a94-26b3-5bea-ae7b-f2fbef1384ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} :answer-field/id #uuid "607dd21d-aeb7-5920-91bc-f439f202ce2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27889ca3-5f85-5eb8-aee5-93c6c3d6c2ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-88298" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =\\underbrace{|\\begin{aligned}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35013c7e-161d-57b9-8b70-9180bf4e5f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d05ac71-25b6-5914-b927-f738d0486d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355052} {:answer/id #uuid "3001b000-bbc1-5019-bdb2-91a649297e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355081} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8d6739-43a1-5e1a-b5bb-e3e9b2ecf2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355224} {:answer/id #uuid "a44c5bce-6f34-5a01-a302-4ac4c7c3863a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356122} {:answer/id #uuid "a87f58bb-1dd0-55ac-8681-4dd4396992db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a44c5bce-6f34-5a01-a302-4ac4c7c3863a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356122} :answer-field/id #uuid "0518f0a3-0881-5d1c-ac25-c50f2ca7e9b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "909b959c-c1db-57e2-bec5-8dd900c2df28" :question/math-academy-id "q-94210" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-7y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and sinusoidal forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{{e}^{7t}}+B{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-\\alpha +6\\beta -7\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\beta -6\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{25}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{4}{25},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35013c7e-161d-57b9-8b70-9180bf4e5f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20626954-1dd4-5be2-abfb-91b616517828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354939} {:answer/id #uuid "27790ecd-b58b-5f7a-a86f-73e12ba0059d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354998} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a703d-5443-5822-8c7f-bdf5f9b97570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355027} {:answer/id #uuid "2c59d3b5-9d2c-5b4e-a801-29a147845b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355042} {:answer/id #uuid "6af718ea-28df-5262-a2c1-7c0d86852384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a703d-5443-5822-8c7f-bdf5f9b97570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355027} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1cb8b1a-c554-5363-8ea8-f95b6c4191c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f527eab-80ea-5448-9f88-66d2121ee8d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94212" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x={e}^{t}.$*" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-3\\alpha +2(\\alpha t+\\beta )=3t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha t+2\\beta -3\\alpha =3t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ 2\\beta -3\\alpha &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{9}{4},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3t}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35013c7e-161d-57b9-8b70-9180bf4e5f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dcb9a18-82de-5ee8-84d6-8e9aa7a186e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354919} {:answer/id #uuid "a3acc217-4cb8-52b5-8ab6-ee3f82f020df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356116} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0357b8-8630-543e-9b50-d8a82d31a117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356478} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f39e99-4e67-5b5f-9891-01a7b54383eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356578} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7a0d5c-694b-5a72-8a6e-267ccdf81003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff7a0d5c-694b-5a72-8a6e-267ccdf81003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356902} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fad42f1-2574-5030-800a-b322e863848e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13373132-2d6b-54b5-b2be-c3279162518a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94215" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x={e}^{t}.$*" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+Bt{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha +(\\alpha t+\\beta )=t+2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha t+\\beta -2\\alpha =t+2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ \\beta -2\\alpha &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =4,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t+4.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88bd4db-8477-5822-9cda-a608e78039e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d7e1bc-5d38-5dbf-bad2-264d5125d334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361016} {:answer/id #uuid "468566b5-2f03-59a0-8fba-5eabe906d457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361141} {:answer/id #uuid "78fd0fdf-3851-5bd0-8b30-f39005a80cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361370} {:answer/id #uuid "98191b6c-4b30-5677-ad67-7b43d32fa55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361513} {:answer/id #uuid "cc39e97d-bf49-51a0-9117-3e75ec5b3d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "468566b5-2f03-59a0-8fba-5eabe906d457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361141} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a96fbcf-71c2-5c3f-be82-f1e508bcdc0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c79b8781-46c0-50e5-a099-68a40298184b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98106" :question/problem "![](images/11/1108dd3f3d7114f40a483edbbb353fdc51062892d75097fbf22cb7879e4a52cb.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "The length of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is equal to the distance between $P$ and $Q.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $P({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $Q({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(3,\\,-2,\\,0)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $\\underbrace{(2,0,-2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\sqrt{(2-3)^{2}+(0-(-2))^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047e3625-67ed-5d88-a08b-5a04e64a3e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360848} {:answer/id #uuid "05a4efbf-a625-5202-aca8-f7ebb98410c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360853} {:answer/id #uuid "2201c2a1-350f-5aee-bd3d-14e254f13a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360986} {:answer/id #uuid "3852b93d-4859-51af-905f-296d60e22b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361093} {:answer/id #uuid "748ceedb-fa5f-522e-abca-04887a6b1390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047e3625-67ed-5d88-a08b-5a04e64a3e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360848} :answer-field/id #uuid "e231751a-b938-582d-87a2-bcb5599c04aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "648fae78-89f1-556f-9670-0284631ad288" :question/math-academy-id "q-98132" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $A(-5,2,9)$ and $B(-1,-2,9)?$" :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A\\underbrace{(-5,2,9)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B\\underbrace{(-1,-2,9)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-1-(-5))^{2}+(-2-2)^{2}+(9-9)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a864a5-90fe-5a19-9ac4-88e0769c548c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360831} {:answer/id #uuid "65d64f3a-edaf-58da-9deb-f28ef214723d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361298} {:answer/id #uuid "a12dda97-cff1-5b8e-8a28-789feebf4648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361557} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ad5876-370c-5c06-a2d7-3697c868e7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361568} {:answer/id #uuid "eaeb134a-5890-53fb-8184-d8779a754096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65d64f3a-edaf-58da-9deb-f28ef214723d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361298} :answer-field/id #uuid "28ad26b4-9c39-5e27-8b07-ab5502f0379b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33cf532b-66bb-5817-8de3-f7bd5092526d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99342" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a50332-95be-5e07-9d81-8b32183161c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360904} {:answer/id #uuid "64fcd74a-108a-502a-aa23-7b7d13b37949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361292} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c04a46-697c-5de1-9e78-234a46b8e932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361574} {:answer/id #uuid "d85bbaff-0ad6-5bb3-8ba8-be01b5088a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361799} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd2aa3f-818d-5ba1-b96e-fca5d1384108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4c04a46-697c-5de1-9e78-234a46b8e932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361574} :answer-field/id #uuid "25d1e6ce-4dee-5f38-be32-5ea8a3c4c6c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6127ecb6-de4f-5077-a618-4d1e1bbd51c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99411" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=4\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e8a11c-8df2-522c-9d68-37338e523245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360838} {:answer/id #uuid "0b7dadcb-324d-5ae6-83d1-1909ec3c2702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360881} {:answer/id #uuid "70ae5295-6ce2-56c7-99ed-046b974429a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361335} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b952f4-df0e-54e9-8f81-8a4186f416b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361595} {:answer/id #uuid "f4dc671e-5c0b-5d36-9b74-34a8b03a45ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b952f4-df0e-54e9-8f81-8a4186f416b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361595} :answer-field/id #uuid "3094debd-288b-541d-8eb3-a03112bc5fc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dbe8acf-27c1-5738-b187-818bf1052532" :question/math-academy-id "q-99418" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2-3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-2+3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232ac2d-f477-56d2-bc66-4e4e7ff088f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360839} {:answer/id #uuid "099c80b1-ec77-53c5-9fec-f0d48316573a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360872} {:answer/id #uuid "554e46a6-951d-57f0-9e37-f12e320d01df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361221} {:answer/id #uuid "63d5d6f1-3790-5286-95bf-cee8cdd5038a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361290} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5cabc7-c549-5a80-a1d9-086dbf2714f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5cabc7-c549-5a80-a1d9-086dbf2714f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361598} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e410a0f-46a7-599e-a5b7-d7ec1ab97b1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "975ead56-2d77-565d-8ac3-aceac4612c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-99428" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1-5\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5190752e-a1c8-5dd8-b834-158e58825739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5190752e-a1c8-5dd8-b834-158e58825739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362541} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bbd186-1d4e-5461-bc79-ce35c2890910" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "231df40f-8e86-536b-af50-43d6f14cbda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-169203" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of $F$ along the border $C$ of the plot $D,$ shown in the diagram above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Circulation $\\,={∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}\\underbrace{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{\\text{Scalar curl of }F}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}\\underbrace{(3x{y}^{2})}_{P}\\,dx+\\underbrace{(-{y}^{3})}_{Q}\\,dy\n$$\n\nIn part 2, we found that the scalar curl of $F$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e150edfa-4d44-5ab4-b043-ba48e414155f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468876} {:answer/id #uuid "4e202e38-3b7c-56bb-b632-db0087893be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e150edfa-4d44-5ab4-b043-ba48e414155f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468876} :answer-field/id #uuid "16b1f541-6829-5760-b5cd-2bf997f751ec" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daff9470-1403-525c-a778-8cd8a3abbaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468878} {:answer/id #uuid "411c8d9b-b9e8-5e53-9add-722476432317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "411c8d9b-b9e8-5e53-9add-722476432317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468879} :answer-field/id #uuid "d99a7be7-c36c-521b-a83f-222874be7a98" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae47d9b-d794-5484-a1b3-59518fd10b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468880} {:answer/id #uuid "52e13f5f-43af-5535-afd2-29e2add95b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ae47d9b-d794-5484-a1b3-59518fd10b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468880} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d70e912-f00f-557a-98ae-ac4eb7d47693" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92a6911e-532d-5f89-96e6-761d0ceb55f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109487" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aedcb32b-f84c-5aa2-b3a4-f7e83746b836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468882} {:answer/id #uuid "aed73398-59c2-51b4-9371-f0f65e98014f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aedcb32b-f84c-5aa2-b3a4-f7e83746b836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468882} :answer-field/id #uuid "9958dde5-b255-5d95-ae9b-baca80f743d5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3915fd0e-f480-5ebd-983a-af8001f5e607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468884} {:answer/id #uuid "23790615-5ac5-5c45-97e2-001e01eff0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3915fd0e-f480-5ebd-983a-af8001f5e607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468884} :answer-field/id #uuid "03f1a276-3cb9-581b-9099-1990fdc1c513" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a54f6205-db26-5c00-8932-ebcca124f521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468886} {:answer/id #uuid "40b5715b-352e-56b1-a124-d548af347d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a54f6205-db26-5c00-8932-ebcca124f521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468886} :answer-field/id #uuid "1659548f-da18-5fc1-9233-0906388e6725" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c9089c-246d-51fb-b493-4538acc22376" :question/math-academy-id "q-109574" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ed2ea04-17c5-525d-bb8b-a73bdb48fa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({B}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362752} {:answer/id #uuid "81d7a34a-2311-5d45-891e-5cd69b89006d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362753} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed9ddc3-ffd8-53ba-802f-ad779bcf8c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({B}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362754} {:answer/id #uuid "7b88adee-5c88-5a21-99be-baee1cf260db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362755} {:answer/id #uuid "7aed3ab2-0476-5b02-8570-9d68a6779e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d7a34a-2311-5d45-891e-5cd69b89006d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362753} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3fad67-e441-547e-bcca-af1012111f8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63c0c3aa-6cd0-5913-9f45-9198721d3066" :question/math-academy-id "q-109850" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a $m\\times n$ matrix. What is the image of $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ under the action of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $B$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{B}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $B$ must be $\\text{Col}(B),$ the column space of $B.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(B)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(B).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ad/ad1557fbd48206baf7239046ada69f60d8cd9ee4916b4f8e37741903ef7d1e9b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab280a44-737b-5327-b8b9-37dc6ef7b3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362772} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d452ec-369b-54d1-8357-79ad02025a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362773} {:answer/id #uuid "99ca8573-a2e6-558c-b3c7-031132e63ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362774} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e2aca3-e8c2-5d00-b0bc-0cf40e3489e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362775} {:answer/id #uuid "887d2b50-662a-542d-9779-a9834c4a9b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3e2aca3-e8c2-5d00-b0bc-0cf40e3489e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362775} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb6ed2ff-8856-5c19-ae37-a59672f92f3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f5df99f-1d28-5975-9513-4422bec5dfb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-109860" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4\\times 6$ matrix. What is the image of ${R}^{4}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $6\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nPre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & {a}_{3}^{T} & {a}_{4}^{T} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{3}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{4}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{4}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the row space of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02554daa-311a-521d-8bb7-985c7d0c82c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363163} {:answer/id #uuid "00588159-608b-585c-b2b5-2b836308f12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363164} {:answer/id #uuid "3a499beb-4e4c-5adf-9db1-91ff32f20121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363165} {:answer/id #uuid "efdc2092-f8a4-5b77-a120-3add5597c886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363166} {:answer/id #uuid "974a9e55-1080-5bb8-aaab-2072bf03f674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02554daa-311a-521d-8bb7-985c7d0c82c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363163} :answer-field/id #uuid "846e397b-48d4-5641-a9ae-b50762794665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd77d9db-96bd-55f7-b4d3-fb162ec30ec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139786" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=20 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-(-2) & =20 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =18 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb9d78f4-aa3e-5f6c-9e00-cb88d8c0a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363170} {:answer/id #uuid "61095090-5603-5eb4-acb6-44d1a726d375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363171} {:answer/id #uuid "54e79a64-d102-5ec7-92fb-704283cbd246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363172} {:answer/id #uuid "d091dad6-c30c-5c5a-93cb-462966115e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363173} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5d8ca3-4cb5-5f45-9f6b-8f05c6c59c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d78f4-aa3e-5f6c-9e00-cb88d8c0a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363170} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e03da2-3c94-5901-94b3-848cec26cc95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1989d3b4-7a5e-5b18-8a4f-6c042b9559dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-139795" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2} &=6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}(3) & =15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =24\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce45e94d-baf5-5098-ac2d-d794c6429bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363219} {:answer/id #uuid "448670c4-632d-5013-a236-b59dc6cbcc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363220} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4bc888-dff7-59f0-a773-62a45b1023fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363221} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa65a2-e9e6-5cda-b206-709db184a16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363222} {:answer/id #uuid "c20f970a-bd81-5297-9fcb-5e40ddbce489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb4bc888-dff7-59f0-a773-62a45b1023fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363221} :answer-field/id #uuid "4547ff35-0e1d-5d8f-b076-6bc2886e7c0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d67166f-3bff-552c-8a7c-19cfbf3fd7f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140900" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}+2{q}_{1}-{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}+2{q}_{1}-{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=-6{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-6+2+3=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a027607c-b49e-5d51-8e9f-696efe340642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363226} {:answer/id #uuid "66dfc0c2-4c41-5b5c-8ca9-8963c9494355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363227} {:answer/id #uuid "92170d95-d6d3-5882-96d9-9f06a90067ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363228} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b6e55-0d08-5b04-9194-57e762fe0902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363229} {:answer/id #uuid "25a800e8-0c9b-5cbf-9949-6d4a63eb0a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66dfc0c2-4c41-5b5c-8ca9-8963c9494355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363227} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f82e91-6709-5adc-99bd-29df5cbf4521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "836a6cda-4c5e-5dc1-9726-f5d184348fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140902" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f9e469-7eec-5401-8986-a4974df572b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363233} {:answer/id #uuid "bbffa08b-7606-549f-bc87-1f500316ebd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363234} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ab06c1-b10b-5935-bf2d-75749c31b94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363235} {:answer/id #uuid "85bd57ed-0fc2-5736-8094-14f443d99ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363236} {:answer/id #uuid "c5eaeb1b-839d-5f69-b76b-0cdf9c511c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85bd57ed-0fc2-5736-8094-14f443d99ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363236} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8043045-7145-51b3-822c-f2d744e6043c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95a58504-d1c2-5301-9dd6-5bea44e00bd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140903" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94bbd4be-9e16-5ff7-86f5-9deb90ba452b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364173} {:answer/id #uuid "c291dfba-6379-5676-a6b4-e3cf0f6b1b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364174} {:answer/id #uuid "99202a3d-788d-55c3-8504-be24ed646fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364175} {:answer/id #uuid "baadede6-09ca-5ce7-809c-fce926ea02ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364176} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f1a527-b1dc-5cc6-a3e9-53c8168ec278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99202a3d-788d-55c3-8504-be24ed646fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364175} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e06997-ff6b-5818-9b5e-fa3e71c896a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0bac4d5-3752-5a5d-b6fe-39c8ef101bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-41485" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=3,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=4,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=2,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-5{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=4{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=2{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-5{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=9{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=5{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=15{q}_{1}+8{q}_{2}+2{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}=15+4+2=21.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74849d77-3f75-57fb-9eda-c7eb146ef089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364180} {:answer/id #uuid "ae17bbad-6406-590f-9037-515c2d2e3302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364181} {:answer/id #uuid "80193887-56ef-5f55-9899-81a4ca2a5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364182} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ed3821-6db7-500c-afa6-af890beb1af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364183} {:answer/id #uuid "ee368a24-e35e-5530-bc4c-c0b11699a84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80193887-56ef-5f55-9899-81a4ca2a5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364182} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd9129a-1c55-5b4e-92c4-8f9a2d89199f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0711fb88-caa5-5f45-81df-dbacaf0d183c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41486" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-16{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=9{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -16 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}=9+2+3=14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6541fae-ca98-5011-918c-a2e113b5f4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364601} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8150ed-2bbb-58ff-9b44-a56ebd9adcf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ide