588-ab63-a01ec7657b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389752} :answer-field/id #uuid "9969359a-3932-5955-a5d7-3796ec7d66c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "001c067c-108b-5fca-a346-5a02614cd60f" :question/math-academy-id "q-175921" :question/problem "$$\n\\underbrace{\\overbrace{3-1=2}^{P}\\,\\overbrace{\\text{and}}^{∧}\\,\\overbrace{2\\text{ is negative}}^{Q}}_{P\\,∧\\,Q}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{or}}_{∨}\\,\\underbrace{\\pi \\text{ is rational}}_{R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P∧Q$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $(P∧Q)∨R$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $P∧Q$ is false, and the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∧Q)∨R & ≡\\overbrace{(\\text{true}∧\\text{false})}^{\\text{false}}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93bc0caa-2352-5d02-9ccf-7267dff4be2c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Compound Statements in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c965136c-a101-5c21-a950-5aab511bb15f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∧" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389770} {:answer/id #uuid "29c7a